values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i053$il$, $il$The same outbound DM is logged twice on the Urs Moeller thread$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-15T11:22:57.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, '{}'::text[], array[$il$i048$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-18T09:02:34.569Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$On Urs Moeller at Coolblue, the Conversation view shows Oliver's opening DM twice. Identical text, both stamped 10 Sep 2026. It was sent once.

SECOND INSTANCE, 17 Sep 2026, Frédéric Gandon at Largo. Touch history shows two Connection request rows on 5 Aug 26, T503 and T817, and the conversation below shows the same connection request note twice, both marked Sent. One request was sent. So this is not one thread, it is a pattern, and the count is worth running now rather than after the LinkedIn export is loaded.

Two possible causes and they need different fixes. The same message ingested twice, which means two touch rows and a dedup rule missing at the point of ingest. Or one touch row rendered twice, which is a display bug and the data is fine. The Largo case argues for the first, because the two rows carry different touch ids. Check the underlying rows first.$il$,
$il$THIRD INSTANCE, AND IT SHOWS WHERE THE DUPLICATES COME FROM. 17 Sep 2026, Hermann-Wilhelm Wantia. His touch history holds five rows and the ids come from four different namespaces:
  20 Aug 26  T855                                          Connection request
  25 Aug 26  agent-8e3a95e2-c1f3-426b-ba5a-f618a50de5a4    Initial message
  25 Aug 26  cr-backfill-f83f6eb8-74a3-48bd-9c2b-8bc7d7912888  Connection request
  25 Aug 26  sup-1787755496481-jewzjs                      Initial message
  26 Aug 26  T901                                          Initial message

FOUR WRITERS, FOUR ID SCHEMES, NO SHARED KEY. A T series, an agent job, a connection request backfill, and something minting sup plus a millisecond timestamp. Each one invents its own identifier, so no two of them can ever recognise the same event. That is not a bug in one importer, it is the absence of the key this item asks for, and it is now visible on screen rather than inferred.

WHAT IS ALMOST CERTAINLY DUPLICATED HERE. The 25 August connection request carries a cr-backfill id and sits five days after the T855 request of 20 August, which strongly suggests a backfill re-recording the same invitation rather than a second one being sent. And three separate Initial message rows on 25 and 26 August, written by three different processes, for what the conversation below shows as one DM on 26 August.

THE CONSEQUENCE IS LIVE ON THIS SCREEN. The draft is labelled message 3 of 3, so the sequence believes this thread is exhausted and is drafting the exit message. If the real history is one connection request and one DM, the exit message is arriving a step or two early, to somebody who has barely heard from us. This is the second time the counter has been provably wrong, after chaser 3 of 2 on i082.

SO THE COUNT IS WORTH RUNNING NOW. Group touches by contact, direction, channel and date, and count the groups with more than one row. Break it down by id prefix as well, because that says which writer is producing them and therefore which one to fix first.

WHY A DUPLICATE TOUCH IS NOT COSMETIC. Touch history is read by things that count. The day seven reply check, the cooldown, the message N of 3 sequence position, and the momentum and volume figures on the dashboard all treat a touch row as one contact event. Two rows for one message means the sequence thinks Oliver is a step further along than he is, and a chaser could fire a position early.

IT ALSO POINTS THE OTHER WAY FROM i048. That item is about touches going MISSING. This is a touch appearing twice. Both are the same underlying problem, which is that nothing owns the identity of a message. A touch needs a stable key, ideally the platform's own message id, so that re-running a sync updates a row rather than adding one. Without that, every future sync or backfill risks doing this again at scale, and the LinkedIn export load in i033 and i048 is exactly such a backfill.

SO THE REAL FIX IS THE KEY, not this thread. Deduplicate on the message identifier rather than on text, because text matching would collapse two genuinely identical short messages sent to different people, and would miss a duplicate that differs by a trailing space.

AND THE KEY HAS TO BE THE SAME KEY FOR EVERY WRITER. A stable identity that the agent, the backfill, the supabase path and the T series all resolve to, so a row can be recognised as already present no matter which process meets it second. Otherwise dedup only ever works inside one writer.$il$);
insert into public.improvement_comments (team_id, improvement_id, who, at, text)
select i.team_id, i.id, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-17T15:35:00.000Z'::timestamptz,
$il$FIFTH ID NAMESPACE, 17 Sept. Dhananjay Choubey at Revent, P303, carries a touch with the id synthetic-cr-P303. So the list is now T numbers, agent jobs, cr-backfill, sup timestamps and synthetic. This one names itself as generated rather than observed.

The underlying connection request does look real, it shows as sent on 30 August in the conversation below, so synthetic is most likely a placeholder id minted where the platform id was unknown. That needs confirming rather than assuming. If any synthetic row describes an event that did not actually happen, then the touch history contains invented touches, and every guard that reads it, cooldown, duplicate approach, day seven check, sequence position, is wrong in the direction that costs the most.

Either way it strengthens the ask: one identity scheme that every writer resolves to, and a flag on each touch saying whether it was observed or inferred.$il$
from public.improvements i where i.team_id = (select id from public.teams limit 1) and i.item_key = $il$i053$il$;
insert into public.improvement_comments (team_id, improvement_id, who, at, text)
select i.team_id, i.id, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-17T16:10:00.000Z'::timestamptz,
$il$THE CLEAREST INSTANCE YET, AND IT SUGGESTS THE MECHANISM. Alexander Peretti at 0815, P595. His touch history holds THREE Initial message rows all dated 28 July 26, ids T775, T576 and T540. The conversation below shows why that is wrong: one InMail on 28 July with a full body, Geraeteschutz in AT und DE, followed by two more 28 July InMail entries that are COMPLETELY EMPTY. One message, three rows, two of them with no content.

THAT POINTS AT A SPECIFIC CAUSE. A row with no body was almost certainly created from a thread listing, where only the metadata is visible, while the row with the body came from opening the thread itself. Two capture paths, no shared key, so the same message lands twice more.

AND IT MAKES THE FIRST CLEANUP CHEAP AND SAFE. Find touch rows with an empty body where another row exists for the same contact, channel and date, and merge them into the one that has content. That does not need the identity scheme to be designed first and it would remove the most obvious duplicates today.

THE COST IS ON SCREEN AGAIN: the footer reads logged as chaser 3 of 2. A sequence cannot be at position three of two, and this is the second contact where that exact string has appeared, after the one on i082. The touch history also opens with synthetic-cr-P595, the same synthetic namespace as Revent.

ONE FURTHER ODDITY WORTH A LOOK, not necessarily this item. The channel counts moved during the day without any send going out: LinkedIn inMail went from 315 to 314 and LinkedIn DM from 191 to 192, with Sent unchanged at 847. That looks like a touch being reclassified from InMail to DM. If something is rewriting the channel on existing touches, that is worth knowing, because channel drives the credit accounting and the free DM logic.$il$
from public.improvements i where i.team_id = (select id from public.teams limit 1) and i.item_key = $il$i053$il$;

