values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i054$il$, $il$A deal belongs to a country, not to a company. Decide the rule before the deal layer is built$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-15T11:45:33.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, '{}'::text[], array[$il$i047$il$]::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Coolblue is headquartered in the Netherlands. Urs Moeller is Country Manager Germany, so the live conversation is a German one. Oliver asked the right question: is the deal Coolblue, or is it Coolblue Germany, and does that mean creating a German entity record.

ANSWER TO THE DIRECT PART FIRST. There is no Coolblue Germany in the lake. Coolblue appears once, C485, coolblue.nl, Deep research done, In conversation. So the German entity does not exist today.

THE RULE WORTH ADOPTING: entity records follow the SIGNATURE, deals follow the MARKET. Create a separate company record only where there is a separate legal entity Pier would actually contract with. Otherwise keep one company and let the deal carry the country. That keeps the account list honest and still lets you run Germany and the Netherlands as separate opportunities with separate volumes, separate timing and separate owners.

For Coolblue that makes it a question for Urs rather than a modelling decision: would a German programme be signed by Coolblue in the Netherlands or by a German entity. It is a fair thing to ask and the answer decides the record.

This needs i047 underneath it. Country level deals without a group structure just produces unlinked look alike companies, which is what is already happening.$il$,
$il$THERE IS A LIVE COMPLIANCE HOLE IN THIS EXACT CASE, and it is the strongest reason to settle the rule now. Coolblue is recorded as Netherlands. Cold email is permitted for Netherlands targeted contacts. Urs is in GERMANY, where outreach is LinkedIn led and cold email is not permitted. A guard reading the COMPANY country would clear an email to him. A guard reading the CONTACT country would stop it. This is exactly the rule written into i038, and Coolblue is the worked example: the legality check must read the contact, falling back to the company only when the contact has no country.

THE SPLIT IS ALREADY HAPPENING BY HAND, INCONSISTENTLY. 35 company records already carry a country in the name: Vodafone Germany and Vodafone Italia, Euronics Deutschland and Euronics Italia, Media Markt Switzerland, Back Market Italia, Lycamobile Germany, Magenta Telekom Austria, Apple Germany. Beyond that, 72 brand names appear more than once. So the convention exists, it is applied to some groups and not others, Coolblue among the nots, and nothing links any of the siblings. Whatever rule gets chosen has to be applied backwards over those 35 as well, or the list stays half and half and neither half can be trusted.

SIZING IS WHERE IT BITES SECOND. If one Coolblue record carries group wide device volumes and a German deal is sized off it, the German opportunity inherits Dutch volume and the number is wrong. Volumes belong at whatever level the deal sits. Same trap as the group roll up warning in i047, now with a real instance attached.

THREE MORE THINGS A COUNTRY LEVEL DEAL NEEDS, worth agreeing with Brad at the same time:
An owner per deal, not per company. Germany is Oliver, the UK is Jack. One company can carry both.
A currency. Euro for Germany, Swiss franc for Switzerland, sterling for the UK. Pier Protect rates already exist in all three, so the deal needs to know which table it is priced from.
Its own stage and next action. A German deal can be in conversation while a Dutch one has not started, and today one company record can only be in one state.

TIMING. The deal layer spec went to Brad on 15 September. This is a requirement of that spec rather than a change to it, so it is worth reaching him before he builds the deal object, not after.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i055$il$, $il$Unresearched company must not stop a draft being written$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-15T12:20:41.000Z'::timestamptz, 2, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$Agreed on the 15 September call. Today the guard refuses to create a draft where the company has not been researched, so contacts sit doing nothing and go stale. Oliver's call: still write the draft, mark it clearly as company not researched, and park it in its own section. He can then override and push it through when he knows something the record does not.

In the Outreach section, a parked draft gets a button that moves it into Pending Review. Same for anything sitting in Refused: a manual route back in, so strict logic never becomes a dead end.$il$,
$il$WHY OLIVER WANTS THE OVERRIDE, in his words: there might be something he has just not put in, or he might have heard something that means now is the time. A guard that cannot be overridden by the person with the most context is a guard that gets worked around.

THE FLAG HAS TO BE ON THE DRAFT ITSELF, not only on the section. The wedge and the angle for an unresearched company are necessarily weaker, so the reviewer needs to know what he is reading before he approves it, not after.

BRAD'S POINT, WORTH KEEPING: once the research agent runs, this mostly solves itself. A contact with a connection request sent should make its company a research priority automatically. So build the override, but treat the queue ordering in i029 as the real fix.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i056$il$, $il$Merge duplicate companies, choosing field by field$il$, $il$Data & schema$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-15T12:21:41.000Z'::timestamptz, 1, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$There is no way to merge two records that are the same company. Recommerce alone appears four times, including one with an exclamation mark in the name from an old character recognition pass. Otto Austria has two. Add a merge action.

It must NOT merge automatically. Oliver's requirement: a preview that goes field by field, because the record with less data overall sometimes holds the one field worth keeping. So: show both values side by side, pick per field, then commit.$il$,
$il$FIELDS THAT WILL CONFLICT and need the per field choice: website, category, country, countries selling in, revenue, employees, research stage, engagement, notes. Contacts and touches should always be unioned rather than chosen between, because losing a touch is losing history.

KEEP THE LOSING ID. After a merge, the absorbed company id should still resolve to the surviving record rather than 404, because that id may be referenced in the lake, in notes, or in something Oliver typed into monday months ago.

SAME CONTROL SERVES i047. Merging is what happens when two records turn out to be the same company; linking is what happens when they are two companies in one group. The UI should make it obvious which one you are doing, or people will merge a subsidiary into its parent and lose it.$il$);

